-- ─── Campbell ───
('wisdom', 'campbell-heros-journey',
 'Campbell: The Call, the Refusal and the Road Back',
 'Joseph Campbell''s map of a thousand stories, used as a lens on the change you are in the middle of',
 'Campbell argued that myths from every culture share one shape: a call, a refusal, a threshold, an ordeal, and a return with something to give. Whether every story truly fits is contested. As a lens on your own change, the map is useful for one thing: it tells you which stage you are in, and that the stage is not the verdict.',
 'promising', 5,
 ARRAY['A way to see a hard stretch as a stage with a next step, not a permanent state','A name for the refusal, the most common place a change quietly dies','The return: a reason to finish that is about what you bring back for someone else'],
 ARRAY['The universality of the monomyth is disputed by folklorists; use it as a lens, not as a law of human nature','Narrative reframing has support in the life-story research; the hero frame specifically has not been trialled, so the chip says promising','A journey frame can inflate ordinary tasks into epics; the map is for real change, not for a Tuesday'],
 E'Joseph Campbell taught comparative mythology at Sarah Lawrence College and in 1949 published The Hero with a Thousand Faces. Its claim is that the world''s myths retell one story, which he called the monomyth: a hero leaves the ordinary world, faces trials, and returns changed with something to give. George Lucas said he wrote Star Wars from it, and screenwriting has used the map ever since.\n\n**The stages that matter**\nCampbell''s full map has seventeen steps; five do the work. The call: something asks you to change. The refusal: you decline, usually with a good reason. The threshold: you cross into unfamiliar territory and cannot easily go back. The ordeal: the hardest part, where the old self does not work any more. The return: you come back with a gift, and the gift is the point.\n\n**Read it honestly**\nFolklorists have argued for decades that the monomyth fits the stories it fits and is forced onto the rest, and that Campbell''s reading is more Jungian than anthropological. Both criticisms are fair. That is why this piece does not ask you to believe that all myths are one. It asks you to use the shape the way a coach uses a training phase: as a name for where you are.\n\n**What the research adds**\nDan McAdams'' work on narrative identity shows that people who tell the story of their hard stretches with a redemptive arc, difficulty that led somewhere, report more wellbeing and generativity than people who tell the same events as contamination stories. That is correlational and the direction is not settled. But it supports the practical move here: naming your stage in a story with a next chapter changes what you do next. The frame itself has not been trialled, so the chip says promising.\n\n**Where it meets your week**\nA program, a new sport, a return after injury, the first weeks of a membership: each has a call, a refusal that sounds like sensible reasons, a threshold and an ordeal. Most people quit at the refusal without ever naming it. The practice below names it.',
 'Most changes die at the refusal, unnamed. Campbell''s map gives the refusal a name and the ordeal a next stage, which is often all the difference between a program abandoned in week three and one finished.',
 ARRAY['Five stages: call, refusal, threshold, ordeal, return. The return is the point.','Use the map as a lens on your own change, not as a law about all stories.','People who tell hard stretches as chapters with a next one do better in the life-story research.'],
 ARRAY['Write the change you are in the middle of, and mark which of the five stages you are in today.','If it is the refusal, write the exact reason you are using, in its own words, and read it back.','Write the return as a gift: who gets something from you having finished, and what.'],
 '[{"author": "Note", "title": "Original lesson on the ideas of Joseph Campbell; not affiliated with the Joseph Campbell Foundation"}, {"author": "Campbell, J.", "title": "The Hero with a Thousand Faces", "year": 1949}, {"author": "McAdams, D. P.", "title": "The psychology of life stories (Rev. Gen. Psychol.)", "year": 2001}, {"author": "Dundes, A.", "title": "Folkloristics in the twenty-first century (J. Am. Folklore), on the limits of the monomyth", "year": 2005}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "Where do most real changes die?", "choices": ["At the ordeal", "At the refusal, usually with a sensible-sounding reason", "At the return"], "correct": 1, "explain": "The refusal is the quiet exit; naming it is the practice."}, {"q": "How should the monomyth be used here?", "choices": ["As proof that all stories are one", "As a lens that names your stage in a change", "As a screenwriting template"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Its universality is contested; its use as a frame for your own change is what this piece keeps."}]'::jsonb,
 325, NULL, 'protocol',
 'campbell',
 'What call have you been refusing, and what is the exact excuse you use?',
 'You named your stage. Did seeing it as a stage, not a verdict, change what you did next?',
 10),

-- ─── Nietzsche ───
('wisdom', 'nietzsche-self-overcoming',
 'Nietzsche: Become Who You Are',
 'Three metamorphoses, one rewrite: from the musts you carry to the will you choose',
 'Nietzsche''s Zarathustra opens with a parable: the spirit becomes a camel that carries every "you must", then a lion that says "I will not", then a child that says "I will" and creates. The philosophy is a stance, not a finding. The practice, rewriting one must as a will, is a way to test whether you chose the load you carry.',
 'speculative', 5,
 ARRAY['A three-step rewrite that separates the duties you chose from the ones you inherited','A name for self-overcoming: the discipline that is hard because it is yours, not because it was assigned','Amor fati as a working stance toward a body, a history and a week you did not pick'],
 ARRAY['Nietzsche is philosophy and rhetoric, not psychology; nothing here is a finding, and the rewrite has no trial evidence, which is why the chip says speculative','His work has been misused for a century as contempt for the weak; self-overcoming is aimed at yourself, never at others','The lion stage, endless refusal, is where some readers stop; the point is the child'],
 E'Friedrich Nietzsche was a professor of classical philology at twenty-four, an invalid by his mid-thirties, and silent after a collapse in 1889. Thus Spoke Zarathustra, written in the 1880s, opens with a short parable he called the three metamorphoses. It is the cleanest summary of what he meant by becoming who you are, the phrase he took from Pindar and put on the cover of his last book.\n\n**Camel, lion, child**\nThe spirit first becomes a camel: it kneels to be loaded, and it takes pride in carrying the heaviest things. Every "you must" of a culture, a family, a scoreboard goes on its back. Then, in the desert, the camel becomes a lion. The lion fights the dragon whose every scale says "thou shalt", and wins the right to say "I will not". But the lion cannot create; it can only refuse. So the lion becomes a child: a new beginning, a game, a wheel rolling from itself, a sacred yes. The child says "I will" and makes something.\n\n**Self-overcoming**\nWhat Nietzsche admired was not strength in the gym sense but the discipline of someone who has chosen their own hardness. He was scathing about people who carried loads assigned to them and called it virtue, and equally scathing about people who stopped at refusal and called it freedom. Becoming who you are is neither: it is the child''s stage, where the will and the act are yours. Amor fati, love of one''s fate, is the stance that goes with it: not resignation, but taking the body, the history and the week you actually have as the material rather than as the excuse.\n\n**Read it honestly**\nNone of this is science, and the practice below has no evidence behind it beyond the plausibility of its mechanism, which resembles the autonomy findings in self-determination theory: goals experienced as one''s own are pursued harder and longer than goals experienced as imposed. That is why the chip says speculative. Keep the exercise; treat the philosophy as a lens, not a result. And read him with the warning his own sister ignored: everything here is aimed at yourself.\n\n**Where it meets your week**\nA streak can be a camel''s load or a child''s game. The same session, done because you must and done because you will, is a different session, and the difference shows up on the days it is hard.',
 'The same streak can be carried like a load or played like a game. Nietzsche''s three stages are a way to find out which one you are doing, and the rewrite is how you move it.',
 ARRAY['Camel carries every must; lion wins the right to refuse; child says I will and creates.','Self-overcoming is chosen hardness, aimed at yourself, never at others.','No trial evidence for the rewrite; the mechanism echoes the autonomy findings, and the chip says so.'],
 ARRAY['List the musts you carry this month: every "I have to" you can hear in your own voice.','For each, write the lion''s line, "I will not", and notice which ones you cannot honestly write.','For one remaining must, write the child''s line, "I will", in your own words, and do it that way today.'],
 '[{"author": "Note", "title": "Original lesson on the ideas of Friedrich Nietzsche; his works are in the public domain"}, {"author": "Nietzsche, F.", "title": "Thus Spoke Zarathustra, Part I, On the Three Metamorphoses", "year": 1883}, {"author": "Kaufmann, W.", "title": "Nietzsche: Philosopher, Psychologist, Antichrist", "year": 1950}, {"author": "Ryan, R. M. & Deci, E. L.", "title": "Self-determination theory and the facilitation of intrinsic motivation, social development, and well-being (Am. Psychol.)", "year": 2000}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "Why is the lion not the final stage?", "choices": ["Because lions are weak", "Because refusal cannot create; the child says I will and makes something", "Because the camel was right"], "correct": 1, "explain": "The lion wins the right to refuse; the point is the child''s sacred yes."}, {"q": "How much evidence supports the rewrite practice?", "choices": ["Strong trial evidence", "None beyond a plausible mechanism, so the chip says speculative", "It is proven by neuroscience"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Philosophy, not findings. Keep the exercise, drop the certainty."}]'::jsonb,
 326, NULL, 'protocol',
 'nietzsche',
 'Which "must" in your life did you never actually choose?',
 'You rewrote one must as a will. Did the action change, or only the sentence?',
 10),

-- ─── Greene ───
('wisdom', 'greene-mastery-apprenticeship',
 'Greene: The Apprenticeship Nobody Skips',
 'Robert Greene on the long, boring, unglamorous phase that every master went through and most people quit',
 'Mastery, published in 2012, studies how people from Darwin to Temple Grandin got good. The common structure is an apprenticeship: years of deep observation, skill acquisition and experimentation before anything original. The practice is a daily deliberate block on one skill, with a feedback loop, for five days.',
 'promising', 5,
 ARRAY['A model of skill that replaces "talent" with a phase you can actually enter','Deliberate practice defined properly: at the edge, with feedback, on the weak part','Social intelligence as part of the craft, not a distraction from it'],
 ARRAY['The 10,000-hour lore that grew around this research overstates it; deliberate practice explains a real but partial share of performance differences, hence promising','Greene''s case studies are narrative reconstructions, not controlled comparisons','An apprenticeship needs a source of feedback; practising without one repeats mistakes fluently'],
 E'Robert Greene writes long books built from biographies, and Mastery is the one where the pattern he found is worth keeping. He followed Darwin, Faraday, Mozart, Temple Grandin, and a set of living practitioners through their careers, and the shape repeats: a life''s task discovered early or late, then an apprenticeship that lasts years, then a creative phase in which the rules learned are broken on purpose. The book''s value is not the biographies. It is the insistence that the apprenticeship cannot be skipped and that almost everyone tries to.\n\n**The three parts of an apprenticeship**\nGreene splits it. Deep observation: before you act, learn how the field really works, its unwritten rules and its people. Skill acquisition: practise the fundamentals past boredom, in the order that lets each skill support the next. Experimentation: begin testing your own ideas while the feedback is still cheap. The order matters. People who experiment first produce novelty without foundations; people who never leave acquisition become fluent imitators.\n\n**Deliberate practice, honestly**\nThe science underneath is Anders Ericsson''s 1993 work on expert performance: what separates the best violinists is not hours played but hours of deliberate practice, meaning effortful work at the edge of ability, on the weak part, with immediate feedback, repeated. The 10,000-hour figure that grew around it was a journalist''s simplification, and a 2014 meta-analysis found deliberate practice explains a meaningful but partial share of performance differences, largest in games and music, smaller in professions. So: the method is real, the number is folklore, and the chip says promising.\n\n**Social intelligence**\nGreene''s most practical chapter is on the people around the work. Mentors compress the apprenticeship; rivals and gatekeepers can end it. He treats reading a room as a skill of the craft, acquired the same way, by observation before action. It is not manipulation. It is the recognition that mastery happens inside a community of practice.\n\n**Where it meets your week**\nYour training program is an apprenticeship with a coach in it. This piece asks you to treat one other craft the same way: the skill that would move it most, thirty minutes at the edge, and a source of feedback that tells you what effort alone hides.',
 'A program is an apprenticeship with the boring parts made visible. Greene shows the same structure under every craft, and the practice puts one of yours on the same footing: at the edge, with feedback, daily.',
 ARRAY['Every master served an apprenticeship: observation, skill acquisition, experimentation, in that order.','Deliberate practice is at the edge, on the weak part, with feedback; the 10,000-hour number is folklore.','Mentors and the people around the work are part of the craft, not a distraction from it.'],
 ARRAY['Name the craft you are actually an apprentice in right now, and the one skill that would move it most.','Design a thirty-minute block on that skill at the edge of your ability, with a way to get feedback the same day.','Run it five days in a row and write one line after each: what the feedback showed that effort alone hid.'],
 '[{"author": "Note", "title": "Original lesson on the ideas of Robert Greene; not affiliated with the author or publisher"}, {"author": "Greene, R.", "title": "Mastery", "year": 2012}, {"author": "Ericsson, K. A., Krampe, R. T. & Tesch-Römer, C.", "title": "The role of deliberate practice in the acquisition of expert performance (Psychol. Rev.)", "year": 1993}, {"author": "Macnamara, B. N., Hambrick, D. Z. & Oswald, F. L.", "title": "Deliberate practice and performance in music, games, sports, education, and professions: a meta-analysis (Psychol. Sci.)", "year": 2014}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "What defines deliberate practice?", "choices": ["Many hours of play", "Effortful work at the edge, on the weak part, with feedback", "Watching experts"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Ericsson''s definition; the hours matter only when they are this kind."}, {"q": "What does the 2014 meta-analysis show about deliberate practice?", "choices": ["It explains everything", "It explains a real but partial share of performance differences", "It explains nothing"], "correct": 1, "explain": "The method is real; the 10,000-hour number is folklore. Promising, not strong."}]'::jsonb,
 327, NULL, 'protocol',
 'greene',
 'In which craft are you still an apprentice while behaving as if you had finished one?',
 'Five days of deliberate practice with feedback. What did the feedback show that effort alone hid?',
 15),

-- ─── Goggins ───
('wisdom', 'goggins-callused-mind',
 'Goggins: Keep the Promises You Make to Yourself',
 'David Goggins'' method separated from the myth: the accountability mirror, the cookie jar, and the limits of the 40% rule',
 'Goggins'' book is a memoir of turning a broken start into ultramarathons and special-forces selections, and it contains three tools that survive without the mythology: a written promise on the mirror, a jar of proof from past hard things, and a habit of doing the thing on the day you least want to. The 40% rule is a heuristic, not a finding, and the piece says so.',
 'promising', 5,
 ARRAY['A daily promise, written and specific, which is the smallest unit of self-trust','The cookie jar: past proof you can reach for at the moment of quitting','A relationship with discomfort that is chosen and bounded, not endless'],
 ARRAY['The 40% rule has no physiological measurement behind it; the psychobiological model of endurance supports the direction, not the number','Goggins trained through injuries that ended in surgeries; his tolerance is not a target and rest is part of the method, whatever the memoir implies','Self-contempt as fuel works short term and corrodes; the promise is kept out of respect, not punishment'],
 E'David Goggins grew up poor and abused, weighed close to 300 pounds in his twenties, and became a Navy SEAL, an ultramarathon runner and a pull-up world-record holder. Can''t Hurt Me, from 2018, is his account of it. It is also a manual, and the manual has a problem: the parts that made him famous, the 40% rule and the callused mind, are heuristics dressed as facts. The tools underneath them are worth keeping, and they are simpler than the myth.\n\n**The accountability mirror**\nAs a young man he wrote his goals on sticky notes on the bathroom mirror and read them every morning: specific, small, dated. Lose the weight. Study for the test. The mechanism is mundane and strong. A promise you can see, that names an action rather than an outcome, is the smallest unit of self-trust, and self-trust is what a streak is built from. Every kept promise makes the next one more likely to be believed.\n\n**The cookie jar**\nWhen he wanted to quit in a race, he reached for a mental jar of proof: past hard things he had already done. Not affirmations. Evidence. The moment of quitting is a moment of forgetting, and the jar is a way of remembering on purpose. It works because the proof is true.\n\n**The 40% rule, honestly**\nGoggins claims that when your mind tells you that you are done, you are at about forty percent of capacity. There is no measurement behind the number. What there is, is Marcora''s psychobiological model of endurance: the point at which people stop is set by perceived effort and motivation, not by the muscle''s actual limit, and both can be moved. So the direction is right, there is more in the tank than the mind reports, and the number is a story. Keep the direction, drop the number. The chip says promising for that reason.\n\n**The part he under-writes**\nGoggins ran through stress fractures and needed multiple surgeries. His tolerance for damage is not a target; it is a warning label. The practice below is bounded: one promise, one hard thing, one day at a time, and rest treated as part of the method rather than as weakness.\n\n**Where it meets your week**\nThe check-in is a promise kept in public to yourself. This piece makes it explicit: written, small, dated, and kept on the day you least want to.',
 'A streak is a stack of kept promises. Goggins'' tools make each one explicit and give you something true to reach for at the moment you want to skip it.',
 ARRAY['A promise to yourself is the smallest unit of self-trust: written, specific, dated.','The cookie jar is evidence, not affirmation; reach for it at the moment of quitting.','More is in the tank than the mind reports; the 40% number is a story, and rest is part of the method.'],
 ARRAY['Write one promise for tomorrow on paper where you will see it in the morning: an action, not an outcome, with a time.','Write three hard things you have already done, in one line each, and keep the list where the promise is.','Tomorrow, keep the promise on the day''s terms, feel like it or not, and note afterwards what the next decision felt like.'],
 '[{"author": "Note", "title": "Original lesson on the ideas of David Goggins; not affiliated with the author or publisher"}, {"author": "Goggins, D.", "title": "Can''t Hurt Me: Master Your Mind and Defy the Odds", "year": 2018}, {"author": "Marcora, S. M. & Staiano, W.", "title": "The limit to exercise tolerance in humans: mind over muscle? (Eur. J. Appl. Physiol.)", "year": 2010}, {"author": "Baumeister, R. F. & Tierney, J.", "title": "Willpower: Rediscovering the Greatest Human Strength", "year": 2011}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "What is the cookie jar?", "choices": ["A reward for finishing", "A list of true past proof to reach for at the moment of quitting", "A motivational quote"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Evidence, not affirmation. It works because it is true."}, {"q": "What does the science say about the 40% rule?", "choices": ["It is measured and exact", "The direction is supported by the psychobiological model; the number is a story", "It is completely false"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Perceived effort sets the stopping point and can be moved; no study produced the number."}]'::jsonb,
 328, NULL, 'protocol',
 'goggins',
 'What is the last promise you made to yourself and broke, and what did you tell yourself afterwards?',
 'You kept one small promise on a day you did not feel like it. What did keeping it change in the next decision?',
 10),

-- ─── Thich Nhat Hanh ───
('wisdom', 'thich-nhat-hanh-one-thing',
 'Thich Nhat Hanh: Wash the Dishes to Wash the Dishes',
 'The Vietnamese monk who taught mindfulness as a way of doing ordinary things, not a way of escaping them',
 'The Miracle of Mindfulness was written as a letter to a friend running a relief programme during the war. Its instruction is simple: when you wash the dishes, wash the dishes. Do the thing you are doing, with the breath as the anchor. The practice is one ordinary task done as the only thing in the world.',
 'strong', 4,
 ARRAY['A form of mindfulness that needs no cushion, no app and no extra time: it is done inside tasks you already do','The breath as a portable anchor you can return to in a queue, a set or an argument','Evidence that this kind of attention training lowers stress and improves mood, from dozens of trials'],
 ARRAY['Mindfulness programmes have solid trial evidence for stress and mood; the effects are moderate, not miraculous, and they need practice to hold','A small minority of people experience unwanted effects from intensive practice; the everyday form here is gentle, and stopping is always allowed','Interbeing and the wider Buddhist frame are a worldview; the practice earns its chip without it'],
 E'Thich Nhat Hanh was a Zen monk from Vietnam who opposed the war from inside it, was exiled for it, and spent the rest of his life teaching in the West what he called engaged Buddhism. The Miracle of Mindfulness began in 1974 as a long letter to a colleague running a school of social workers in the war zone, and it is still the clearest short manual on the subject.\n\n**Doing the thing you are doing**\nThe central image is washing dishes. There are two ways to do it: to get them done so you can have your tea, or to wash them. In the first, you are not present for the dishes and, he argues, you will not be present for the tea either, because the habit of being elsewhere follows you. Mindfulness, in his usage, is simply being where you are, doing what you are doing, and the everyday tasks are where it is trained, not the cushion.\n\n**The breath as anchor**\nEvery time attention wanders, and it will, the breath is where you come back to. Not a special breath: the one already happening. He recommends counting or a short phrase paired with the in-breath and out-breath, and a bell, any bell, as a reminder to stop and take three breaths. The point is not to breathe better. It is to have a place to return to that is always with you.\n\n**What the research adds**\nMindfulness training has been studied more than any other contemplative practice. A 2014 review in JAMA Internal Medicine found moderate evidence that mindfulness programmes improve anxiety, depression and pain, and low-to-moderate evidence for stress and quality of life. Later meta-analyses on healthy adults point the same way for stress. The programmes studied are usually structured eight-week courses; the everyday form here is gentler and the effects should be expected to be smaller. The mechanism, though, is the same trained attention, and the practice has enough behind it for a strong chip.\n\n**The half-smile and the warning**\nHe recommended a half-smile during practice, which sounds precious and works: it is difficult to hold a small smile and stay tense. He also knew that people can be flooded by what they find when they stop moving. If that happens, stop; the practice is meant to be kind.\n\n**Where it meets your week**\nA set of squats, a walk to the station, the dishes after dinner. Each is already in your day. One of them, done as the only thing, is the whole practice.',
 'Meditation on the check-in is a box. This piece is the version that lives inside tasks you already do, so the box gets ticked by the way you wash a plate or walk to the gym.',
 ARRAY['Do the thing you are doing; the habit of being elsewhere follows you everywhere.','The breath is the anchor because it is always with you; a bell is a reminder to return to it.','Mindfulness programmes have moderate trial evidence for stress and mood; the everyday form is gentle and portable.'],
 ARRAY['Choose one task you do today anyway and rush through: the dishes, a walk, brushing your teeth.','Do it as the only thing in the world: hands, sounds, temperature, the breath underneath, and return to the breath each time you drift.','Set a bell, any timer, for one moment in the day, and take three slow breaths when it rings.'],
 '[{"author": "Note", "title": "Original lesson on the ideas of Thich Nhat Hanh; not affiliated with Plum Village or his publishers"}, {"author": "Thich Nhat Hanh", "title": "The Miracle of Mindfulness", "year": 1975}, {"author": "Goyal, M. et al.", "title": "Meditation programs for psychological stress and well-being: a systematic review and meta-analysis (JAMA Intern. Med.)", "year": 2014}, {"author": "Britton, W. B. et al.", "title": "Defining and measuring meditation-related adverse effects in mindfulness-based programs (Clin. Psychol. Sci.)", "year": 2021}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "Where, in this teaching, is mindfulness trained?", "choices": ["Only on the cushion", "Inside ordinary tasks, by doing the thing you are doing", "In retreats"], "correct": 1, "explain": "The dishes are the practice; the cushion is optional."}, {"q": "What does the trial evidence show for mindfulness programmes?", "choices": ["Cures for most conditions", "Moderate improvements in anxiety, depression, pain and stress", "No measurable effect"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Moderate, repeated, real. The everyday form should be expected to do a little less."}]'::jsonb,
 329, NULL, 'protocol',
 'thich-nhat-hanh',
 'Which daily task do you rush through as if it were an obstacle to your actual life?',
 'You did one task fully. What did you notice in it that you had never noticed before?',
 5),

-- ─── Kabat-Zinn ───
('wisdom', 'kabat-zinn-full-catastrophe',
 'Kabat-Zinn: Mindfulness as Medicine',
 'The molecular biologist who put meditation in a hospital, measured it, and gave the world an eight-week course',
 'In 1979 Jon Kabat-Zinn opened a stress-reduction clinic in a Massachusetts hospital basement for patients medicine had run out of options for. The programme he built, MBSR, is the most-studied mindfulness course in existence. Its core is the body scan: attention moved slowly through the body without trying to change anything.',
 'strong', 5,
 ARRAY['The body scan: a ten-minute practice that restores contact with a body most of us only notice when it hurts','Stress reactivity versus stress response: a distinction you can feel once you have the practice','The best-evidenced form of mindfulness there is, stripped of any religious frame'],
 ARRAY['MBSR shows moderate effects on stress, anxiety and depression in meta-analyses; it is not a cure and not a replacement for treatment','Lying-down practice invites sleep; that is fine, and it is also not the exercise','A minority report unwanted effects with intensive practice; this piece uses the short form and permission to stop'],
 E'Jon Kabat-Zinn had a PhD in molecular biology from MIT and a long personal practice of Zen and yoga when, in 1979, he persuaded the University of Massachusetts Medical Center to let him run a course for patients with chronic pain and stress-related illness. He called it the Stress Reduction and Relaxation Program, later Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction, and he did two unusual things: he removed every trace of religion, and he measured the results. Full Catastrophe Living, from 1990, is the course in book form. The title is from Zorba the Greek, who is asked whether he has ever been married and answers that he has a wife, a house, children, the full catastrophe.\n\n**The body scan**\nMBSR''s first practice is the body scan. Lying down, you move attention slowly from the toes to the top of the head, region by region, noticing whatever is there, warmth, pressure, nothing, an ache, without trying to relax it or change it. The instruction that makes it different from relaxation exercises is the last one: you are not trying to make anything happen. You are practising being in contact with the body as it is. Most people discover regions they had stopped feeling.\n\n**Reactivity and response**\nKabat-Zinn''s model of stress distinguishes the automatic reaction, in which a stressor triggers the full cascade before you have noticed it, from a response, in which awareness enters between the trigger and the cascade and gives you a choice. The body scan trains the noticing. The rest of the course applies it to breathing, walking, eating and, eventually, to the difficult conversation and the diagnosis.\n\n**What the research adds**\nMBSR has been tested in hundreds of trials. A 2015 meta-analysis in healthy adults found moderate effects on stress and smaller ones on anxiety, depression and quality of life. Reviews in clinical populations find similar moderate benefits for anxiety and depression. Effects are real, repeatable and not large; they hold better with continued practice. The programme is eight weeks with 45 minutes a day of homework; the ten-minute scan below is the entry to it, not the whole course, and it still earns a strong chip because the technique and the evidence are the same.\n\n**Where it meets your week**\nRPE, soreness, how you slept: the check-in asks you to read the body every day. The scan is how you train the instrument you are reading with.',
 'The check-in asks you to read your body every day. The body scan trains the reading, and it is the single best-evidenced mindfulness practice available.',
 ARRAY['The body scan: attention moved through the body without trying to change anything.','Awareness between the trigger and the cascade turns a stress reaction into a response.','MBSR has moderate, repeatable effects on stress and mood across hundreds of trials.'],
 ARRAY['Lie down where you will not be interrupted for ten minutes and set a timer.','Move attention from the toes upward, region by region, naming what is there without adjusting it; when you drift, return to the last region.','Finish by feeling the whole body at once for three breaths, then note which region had gone quiet before you visited it.'],
 '[{"author": "Note", "title": "Original lesson on the ideas of Jon Kabat-Zinn; not affiliated with the author or the Center for Mindfulness"}, {"author": "Kabat-Zinn, J.", "title": "Full Catastrophe Living: Using the Wisdom of Your Body and Mind to Face Stress, Pain, and Illness", "year": 1990}, {"author": "Khoury, B. et al.", "title": "Mindfulness-based stress reduction for healthy individuals: A meta-analysis (J. Psychosom. Res.)", "year": 2015}, {"author": "Goldberg, S. B. et al.", "title": "Mindfulness-based interventions for psychiatric disorders: A systematic review and meta-analysis (Clin. Psychol. Rev.)", "year": 2018}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "What separates the body scan from a relaxation exercise?", "choices": ["It is longer", "You are not trying to make anything happen, only to be in contact with what is there", "It uses music"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Contact, not control. Regions you had stopped feeling come back."}, {"q": "What size of effect does MBSR show in meta-analyses?", "choices": ["Large and curative", "Moderate on stress, smaller on anxiety and depression", "None"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Real, repeatable, moderate. Held better with continued practice."}]'::jsonb,
 330, NULL, 'protocol',
 'kabat-zinn',
 'Where does stress show up in your body first, before you have called it stress?',
 'Ten minutes of scanning. Which part of the body that you had stopped feeling came back?',
 10),

-- ─── Attia ───
('wisdom', 'attia-centenarian-decathlon',
 'Attia: Train for the Last Decade',
 'Peter Attia''s reframe of exercise: choose what you want to be able to do at ninety, then back-calculate the training',
 'Outlive argues that healthspan, the years you can still do what you care about, is the target, and that most people train for the next race instead of the last decade. The Centenarian Decathlon is the tool: ten physical tasks you want at ninety, mapped to the capacities that make them possible, trained now.',
 'strong', 5,
 ARRAY['A reason to train that survives the day motivation does not: a specific future you can picture','The four capacities that predict a good last decade: aerobic base, peak aerobic capacity, strength, stability','Some of the strongest mortality data in medicine, translated into what to do on Tuesday'],
 ARRAY['Fitness and strength predict mortality strongly in cohorts; trials cannot run for decades, so the causal claim is inferred, well supported and still inferred','Attia''s clinical protocols go far beyond exercise into testing and medication; this piece keeps only the training frame','Training for ninety at thirty can be used to postpone living; the decathlon is a reason to train, not a reason to wait'],
 E'Peter Attia is a physician who left surgery and a stretch at McKinsey to practise what he calls Medicine 3.0: prevention aimed decades ahead. Outlive, from 2023, is the argument and the manual. Its central move for training is a question: what do you want to be able to do in your last decade? Lift a grandchild. Carry groceries up stairs. Get off the floor without hands. Walk a trail. Most people have never asked, so they train for the next event and arrive at eighty unable to do the things they would have chosen.\n\n**The Centenarian Decathlon**\nThe tool is a list. Write ten physical tasks you want to be able to do at ninety, specifically. Then back-calculate. Capacity declines with age at a known rate; to do a task at ninety you need a large margin now, so each task maps to a capacity to build well beyond the task itself. Carrying two bags of groceries up three flights at ninety needs the grip, the legs and the aerobic base of someone who can farmer-carry far more than that at forty.\n\n**Four capacities**\nAttia trains four things and ranks them by evidence. Aerobic efficiency, zone 2, the base that everything else sits on. Peak aerobic capacity, VO2max, the single strongest fitness predictor of mortality in the cohort data. Strength, especially grip, carrying and the legs, which predicts mortality nearly as well. And stability: the ability to load the body safely, which is what lets you keep training the other three into old age instead of getting hurt out of them.\n\n**What the research adds**\nA 2018 study of over 120,000 people found that cardiorespiratory fitness was inversely associated with mortality across the whole range, with no upper limit to the benefit and the least fit at risk comparable to smoking. Meta-analyses of muscular strength find the same direction: stronger people die later, across ages and causes. These are cohorts, not trials; nobody can randomise forty years of training. The consistency, size and dose-response make the inference as strong as observational medicine gets, and the practice earns a strong chip.\n\n**Where it meets your week**\nYour program trains for the next block. This piece asks what it is missing for the last decade, and adds one session for the capacity it ignores.',
 'The program trains for the next block. Attia asks what you want your body to do in its last decade, and the answer usually reveals one capacity your training is quietly ignoring.',
 ARRAY['Train for the last decade: ten specific tasks at ninety, back-calculated to capacities now.','Four capacities: aerobic base, VO2max, strength, stability. VO2max and strength predict mortality strongly.','Cohort evidence, not trials, and as strong as observational medicine gets.'],
 ARRAY['Write ten physical things you want to be able to do at ninety, specific enough to picture.','Beside each, write the capacity it needs most: aerobic base, peak aerobic, strength or stability.','Find the capacity your current week trains least and add one session for it, starting this week.'],
 '[{"author": "Note", "title": "Original lesson on the ideas of Peter Attia; not affiliated with the author or Early Medical"}, {"author": "Attia, P. with Gifford, B.", "title": "Outlive: The Science and Art of Longevity", "year": 2023}, {"author": "Mandsager, K. et al.", "title": "Association of cardiorespiratory fitness with long-term mortality among adults undergoing exercise treadmill testing (JAMA Netw. Open)", "year": 2018}, {"author": "García-Hermoso, A. et al.", "title": "Muscular strength as a predictor of all-cause mortality in an apparently healthy population: a systematic review and meta-analysis (Arch. Phys. Med. Rehabil.)", "year": 2018}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "What is the Centenarian Decathlon?", "choices": ["A race for old people", "Ten physical tasks you want at ninety, back-calculated to the capacities to train now", "A supplement stack"], "correct": 1, "explain": "A reason to train that is specific enough to picture and to plan from."}, {"q": "What kind of evidence links fitness and strength to mortality?", "choices": ["Randomised trials", "Large cohorts with consistent dose-response, as strong as observational medicine gets", "Anecdotes"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Nobody can randomise forty years of training; the inference is well supported and still an inference."}]'::jsonb,
 331, NULL, 'protocol',
 'attia',
 'What do you want to be able to do at ninety that you are not training for at all?',
 'You added one session for the capacity you ignored. Did it feel like training or like insurance, and which one keeps you doing it?',
 15),

-- ─── Jung, second piece ───
('wisdom', 'jung-persona-mask',
 'Jung: The Persona and the Price of the Mask',
 'The face you show the world is necessary. Mistaking it for yourself is the mistake Jung spent a career describing',
 'Jung called the persona the mask a person wears to meet the world: a role, a rank, a reputation. It is useful, and it becomes a problem when the mask is mistaken for the face. Individuation, his word for becoming whole, begins with telling the two apart. The practice is a plain inventory of where the mask is doing the living.',
 'promising', 5,
 ARRAY['A clear line between the useful role and the identification that costs you','The midlife pattern Jung described, which shows up in anyone who has climbed a ladder','Individuation as an ordinary practice: small acts done as yourself rather than as the role'],
 ARRAY['Jungian theory is not experimental psychology; the persona is a model, and the practice earns its promising chip from the self-concept and self-discrepancy literature, not from Jung','Dropping a role too fast is its own drama; the practice is an inventory, not a resignation letter','The persona is necessary; the aim is a mask you can take off, not no mask'],
 E'Carl Jung split from Freud in 1913 and spent the next half-century mapping the parts of the psyche that are not the ego. The shadow is the famous one. The persona is the quieter one, and for people who are good at what they do it is often the more important. The word is the Latin for an actor''s mask, and Jung meant it neutrally: the persona is the face you build to meet the world, shaped by what your work, your family and your culture reward.\n\n**Necessary, and not you**\nJung was clear that the persona is not a lie. You need one. A surgeon needs a surgeon''s face; a coach needs a coach''s. The trouble starts when the person and the persona fuse, when someone is their rank, their record, their reputation, and has nothing left when those are threatened. He called it identification with the persona, and he thought it was the characteristic illness of successful people at midlife: the ladder climbed, the mask perfected, and a hollowness behind it that no further success touches.\n\n**Individuation**\nHis word for the way out was individuation: becoming the whole of what you are, which includes the shadow and excludes nothing. It is not self-improvement. It is closer to self-inclusion, and it begins with the plain act of noticing where the mask is doing the living. Which decisions are made because the role requires them? Which feelings are not allowed because the role would not have them? Which people only ever meet the mask?\n\n**What the research adds**\nJung''s theory is not experimental, and this piece does not pretend otherwise. What has been studied is the gap between the self as presented and the self as felt: self-discrepancy work since Higgins in 1987 links large gaps between the ought self and the actual self to anxiety and low mood, and the authenticity literature links acting in line with one''s own values to wellbeing. Those findings support the direction of the practice, not Jung''s architecture. The chip says promising for that reason.\n\n**Where it meets your week**\nA tier, a division, a badge case: the app hands you a persona if you want one. The Watts and Tolle lessons ask you to hold it lightly. This one asks the prior question: where, this week, did the mask decide?',
 'Ranks and tiers are a persona the app can hand you. Jung''s question is the one under Tolle''s and Watts'': not whether to hold the role lightly, but where it has already started deciding for you.',
 ARRAY['The persona is the necessary face you meet the world with; the illness is mistaking it for yourself.','Identification with the role is the midlife pattern of successful people: the ladder climbed, the hollowness behind it.','Individuation starts with an inventory of where the mask is doing the living; the self-discrepancy research supports the direction.'],
 ARRAY['List three decisions you made this week and, beside each, write who made it: you, or the role.','Write one feeling the role does not allow you, and where it went instead.','Do one small thing today as yourself rather than as the role, and tell nobody.'],
 '[{"author": "Note", "title": "Original lesson on the ideas of Carl Jung; not affiliated with the Jung estate or any Jungian institute"}, {"author": "Jung, C. G.", "title": "Two Essays on Analytical Psychology (Collected Works 7), on the persona", "year": 1928}, {"author": "Higgins, E. T.", "title": "Self-discrepancy: A theory relating self and affect (Psychol. Rev.)", "year": 1987}, {"author": "Kernis, M. H. & Goldman, B. M.", "title": "A multicomponent conceptualization of authenticity: Theory and research (Adv. Exp. Soc. Psychol.)", "year": 2006}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "What is the persona, in Jung''s sense?", "choices": ["A lie you tell", "The necessary face you build to meet the world, which becomes a problem when mistaken for yourself", "The shadow"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Neutral and needed; the illness is identification with it."}, {"q": "What research supports the direction of the practice?", "choices": ["Jung''s case studies", "Self-discrepancy and authenticity findings on the gap between the presented and felt self", "Brain scans of the persona"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Jung''s architecture is a model; the gap between ought and actual self has been measured."}]'::jsonb,
 332, NULL, 'protocol',
 'jung',
 'Which decision this week did the role make for you, and would you have made it as yourself?',
 'You did one thing as yourself, not as the role, and told nobody. What was different about doing it unwitnessed?',
 10),

-- ─── The map ───
('wisdom', 'how-to-walk-a-path',
 'How to Walk a Path',
 'The Vault''s loop and its map: six paths, twenty teachers, one rule for turning an idea into a week',
 'Every piece in this library now runs the same loop: understand the idea, answer one question privately, run one bounded practice, answer one question afterwards. Paths string pieces into a change. This piece is the manual: how the loop works, what the map is for, and why the answers stay yours.',
 'strong', 3,
 ARRAY['The loop, explained once: understand, reflect, practise, integrate','The six paths and which dimension of a life each one works on','The privacy rule: reflections are yours and are never shown to anyone'],
 ARRAY['A path is a suggested order, not a course you owe anyone; skipping is allowed','Running several paths at once is the fastest way to run none; one at a time','The loop rewards practice, not reading; a piece read and not practised is a piece not yet used'],
 E'The old Vault was a library: shelves of pieces, each graded by evidence, each ending in a quiz and a button that said complete. Reading was the whole loop. That is fine for a protocol, sleep timing, protein dosing, where knowing the number is most of the work. It is not enough for an idea. An idea from Frankl or Epictetus is not used by being understood; it is used by being run on a Tuesday and looked at afterwards.\n\n**The loop**\nEvery piece with a question at the end of it now runs four stages. Understand: read the idea, take the quiz if you want the check. Reflect: one question, answered in your own words, before you do anything. Practise: one bounded exercise with a time on it, most under fifteen minutes, some meant to run over days. Integrate: one question afterwards, about what actually happened. The practice is what earns progress and XP; the reading on its own earns nothing, on purpose. Fifteen XP for the first practice of each day, and no more, so the loop can add to the ladder without becoming a way to farm it.\n\n**The map**\nTwenty teachers and thinkers sit behind the pieces, each with a lens: what they let you see. Tap a name to see their ideas. Six paths string pieces into a change, one for each dimension of a life this app cares about: the body and its long game, the mind and its presence, discipline, character, purpose, mastery. A path is a suggested order. The next piece is always the first one you have not practised.\n\n**Today**\nHome and the Vault both open with one practice for today, chosen from what your week looks like: fewer check-ins than usual points at systems, a heavy training week with short sleep points at the long game, a strong meditation week points deeper into presence, a long streak points at meaning. It is a pointer, not a diagnosis. The whole map is always one tap away.\n\n**Your answers are yours**\nReflections are stored so you can read them back. They are never shown on the feed, the boards, a profile or to another member, and the coach never quotes them. Achievements for practices run are public like every other badge; the words are not.\n\n**One rule**\nOne piece, one practice, one path at a time. A path finished is a week changed, not a list completed.',
 'A library is used by reading. A map is used by walking. This piece is the difference, and the rule that keeps the Vault a place you use rather than a place you visit.',
 ARRAY['Understand, reflect, practise, integrate: practice earns progress, reading alone does not.','Six paths, one per dimension; the next piece is the first one you have not practised.','Reflections are private and stay that way; only practices run become achievements.'],
 ARRAY['Open the path that Today points you at and read its first piece.','Answer the reflection before the practice, in your own words, without editing.','Run the practice today, and answer the integration question only after you have.'],
 '[{"author": "Note", "title": "The Vault''s own manual for the practice loop and the map; no external source"}, {"author": "Lally, P. et al.", "title": "How are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world (Eur. J. Soc. Psychol.)", "year": 2010}, {"author": "Gollwitzer, P. M. & Sheeran, P.", "title": "Implementation intentions and goal achievement: A meta-analysis of effects and processes (Adv. Exp. Soc. Psychol.)", "year": 2006}]'::jsonb,
 '[{"q": "What earns progress in the Vault?", "choices": ["Reading a piece", "Running its practice", "Opening the app"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Understanding is the first stage; the practice is the one that counts."}, {"q": "Who can see your reflections?", "choices": ["Your squad", "Only you", "The coach quotes them in chat"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Private rows, never surfaced; only practices run become badges."}]'::jsonb,
 319, NULL, 'foundations',
 NULL,
 'Which of the six dimensions, body, mind, discipline, character, purpose, mastery, has had the least of your attention this year?',
 'You walked the first piece of a path. What did the loop ask of you that the old reading did not?',
 5)
